          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Reedy model structure
            is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+model+structure+on+functors"} global model structure on functors
          %p
            given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the
            %strong Reedy model structure
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [R,C] = Func(R,C)
          %p
            As opposed to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+model+structure+on+functors"} projective and injective model structure
            on functors this does not require any further structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            If all three exist, then, in a precise sense, the Reedy model structure sits
            %em in between
            the injective and the projective model structure. As such, it has the advantage that
            %em both
            the cofibrations as well as the fibrations can be fairly explicitly described and detected in terms of cofibrations and fibrations in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
